[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Try-with-Resources Statement (Automatic Resource Management)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `try-with-resources` statement is a `try` statement that declares one or more resources. A resource is an object that must be closed after the program is finished with it. The `try-with-resources` statement ensures that each resource is closed at the end of the statement. Any object that implements the `java.lang.AutoCloseable` interface, which includes all objects which implement `java.io.Closeable`, can be used as a resource."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of Try-with-Resources"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Simplifies resource management by automatically closing resources."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reduces boilerplate code associated with manually closing resources in `finally` blocks."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Helps prevent resource leaks, which can lead to performance issues or system instability."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Syntax"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The syntax of the `try-with-resources` statement is as follows:"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"try (ResourceType resource1 = new ResourceType();
     ResourceType resource2 = new ResourceType()) {
    // Use the resources
} catch (ExceptionType e) {
    // Handle exceptions
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, `ResourceType` is the class of the resource, `resource1` and `resource2` are the resource variables, and `ExceptionType` is the type of exception that can be thrown within the `try` block.  Multiple resources can be declared within the `try` block's parentheses, separated by semicolons. The resources are closed in the reverse order of their declaration."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Reading from a File"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example demonstrates reading from a file using `try-with-resources`. The `BufferedReader` is automatically closed after the `try` block completes, regardless of whether an exception is thrown."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.io.IOException;

public class TryWithResourcesExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String fileName = "example.txt";

        try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(fileName))) {
            String line;
            while ((line = br.readLine()) != null) {
                System.out.println(line);
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("IOException: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `BufferedReader` `br` is declared within the `try-with-resources` statement. The `BufferedReader` will be automatically closed when the `try` block finishes, even if an `IOException` is thrown. This eliminates the need for a `finally` block to close the reader."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Writing to a File"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example demonstrates writing to a file using `try-with-resources`. The `BufferedWriter` is automatically closed after the `try` block completes."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"import java.io.BufferedWriter;
import java.io.FileWriter;
import java.io.IOException;

public class TryWithResourcesWriteExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String fileName = "output.txt";

        try (BufferedWriter bw = new BufferedWriter(new FileWriter(fileName))) {
            bw.write("This is a line of text.\n");
            bw.write("This is another line of text.\n");
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("IOException: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        System.out.println("File writing complete.");
    }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Multiple Resources"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example demonstrates using multiple resources within a single `try-with-resources` statement.  Both the `BufferedReader` and `BufferedWriter` are automatically closed."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.BufferedWriter;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.io.FileWriter;
import java.io.IOException;

public class TryWithResourcesMultiple {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String inputFile = "input.txt";
        String outputFile = "output.txt";

        try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(inputFile));
             BufferedWriter bw = new BufferedWriter(new FileWriter(outputFile))) {

            String line;
            while ((line = br.readLine()) != null) {
                bw.write(line + "\n");
            }

        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("IOException: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        System.out.println("File copy complete.");
    }
}"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `BufferedReader` and `BufferedWriter` are declared within the `try-with-resources` statement. The resources are closed in the *reverse* order of their declaration. So `BufferedWriter` is closed first, and then `BufferedReader`."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Suppressed Exceptions"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"If an exception is thrown both in the `try` block and during the automatic closing of a resource, the exception thrown in the `try` block is the one that is propagated. The exception thrown during resource closing is *suppressed*. You can access these suppressed exceptions using the `getSuppressed()` method of the primary exception."},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"import java.io.FileOutputStream;
import java.io.IOException;

public class SuppressedExceptions {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (FileOutputStream fos = new FileOutputStream("test.txt")) {
            // Simulate an exception during the try block
            throw new IOException("Exception in try block");
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Caught exception: " + e.getMessage());
            Throwable[] suppressed = e.getSuppressed();
            if (suppressed.length > 0) {
                System.err.println("Suppressed exceptions:");
                for (Throwable t : suppressed) {
                    System.err.println("  " + t.getMessage());
                }
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, an `IOException` is deliberately thrown within the `try` block. Additionally, if the `FileOutputStream` fails to close properly (e.g., due to disk errors), it might throw another `IOException` during the implicit `close()` call. The exception thrown by the `FileOutputStream.close()` method would be added as a suppressed exception to the original exception."},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `try-with-resources` statement is a powerful feature in Java that simplifies resource management and helps prevent resource leaks. By automatically closing resources, it reduces boilerplate code and improves the reliability of your applications. Always use `try-with-resources` when working with resources that implement the `AutoCloseable` interface."}]
